WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.500
Josh: Balance in video games is always a contentious issue and players are quick

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.120
to voice their opinions on any changes
developers make especially when it

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.840
affects them personally. When something
is made weaker in a video game we call

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.360
it a "nerf", but where did that expression
come from and why has the term gained

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.380
such massive popularity? To find out we
have to travel back in time to 1997.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.980
The easiest just got easier.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:27.720
Instant messages.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.360
I can customize my e-mail.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.300
My niece sent me a picture.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.280
If you have a phone line, you can be online.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.840
It's the easiest way to keep up with old
friends.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.880
Everyone I know is on it.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.380
Josh: It's hard to overstate how

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.600
different the internet was back then.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.000
About 37% of American households had

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.660
home computers and only 18 percent of
them had internet access.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.520
Along with the first cloned sheep,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.800
Starfox 64, and 56k
modems, 1997 saw the release of

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.680
Ultima Online, a massively multiplayer online role-playing game or MMORPG.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.680
It was a new breed of game,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.920
one that offered possibilities unlike its predecessors

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.080
and because of the persistent world, it
also had to adapt to its players.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.240
Richard Garriott: It was very broken upon launch and

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.320
one of the first things is we're here

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.450
scrambling to get to catch-up with stuff
we realized that people are both really

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.080
upset and really passionate about being there.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.200
You know, if they were

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.140
upset enough they could just quit and go
away and go somewhere else.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.580
But these people weren't quitting and going away.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.220
They cared. They cared, they were in the game

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.380
protesting in the game.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.400
At one point in Ultima Online, swords were toned down

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.120
be a little less effective.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.620
The response from some players was that they felt

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.460
like Nerf weapons instead of actual
blades.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.200
The term "nerf" soon became

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.060
shorthand for anything that was rendered
less effective due to a game update and

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.020
it makes a lot of sense.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.200
It's easier to say it was "nerfed" instead of saying

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.160
"Balance changes made my character
weaker," or something like that.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.340
But it's easy to forget that there was a time when nerfs

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.440
were a lot more difficult to implement.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.840
Titles like Street Fighter 3
which had long-lived competitive scenes

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.040
had to release completely new versions
of the game to change anything.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.060
Even so, the last version of the game Third Strike had two extremely overpowered

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.160
characters in Ken and Chun-Li.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.480
Had Capcom been able to patch the game, would we

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.880
have seen the ever iconic EVO Moment 37?

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.960
Ken and Chun-Li would have been obvious

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.340
targets for nerfs, so who knows?

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.920
Today the Internet

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.680
has made patching games easier than ever.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.880
That means games can be updated more

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.180
frequently, but it also means that nerfs
are inescapable.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.880
Jay: Well, f**k that!

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.700
The fact that balance changes are not always
explained and that developers often have

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:58.940
differing views of the game's balance
than players has also led to the term's

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.680
development as a meme.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.380
In early League of Legends,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.860
the champion Irelia was nerfed over and over.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.680
While other champions
perceived as stronger

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.120
weren't changed as quickly.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.460
This gave rise to the meme,

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.040
"Better Nerf Irelia", a sarcastic response

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.940
to basically anything.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.960
Implying that Riot would nerf the champion for

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.580
illogical reasons,

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:20.160
or even no reason at all.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.240
Rather than nerfing the champions that needed it.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.900
Sometimes though memes don't
represent reality.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.800
Though Better Nerf Irelia stuck around,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.300
the champion didn't
actually suffer that many nerfs after

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:37.860
the early years of LoL – a fact that TSM's
TheOddOne discovered on stream.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.400
TheOddOne: You always nerf us!

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.180
When was the last time Irelia has even been nerfed?

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:42.900
I'm gonna look it up.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.220
Let's go look it up right now...

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.960
What?

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.060
What?!

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.980
She wasn't even f**king – hasn't even
been f**king nerfed!

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.340
Holy shit. Irelia players are geniuses.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.740
Like, she doesn't even get f**kin' nerfed.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.340
It's all been buffs...

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.960
Some game developers like Blizzard Entertainment

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:05.720
haven't shied away from community
sensitivity around character balancing.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.520
In Overwatch, they've even gone as far
as acknowledging it in game

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.060
with self-referential humor.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.420
I reached out to Hasbro to find out how they felt about

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:22.400
the Nerf trademark being used to
describe things that were made worse.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.340
Unsurprisingly, they didn't respond.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.500
Although, I did find a Nerf dart

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.260
in my driveway the other day.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.840
So there you have it.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:35.180
Nerfs – now an inescapable fact of gaming life.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.600
Here's to hoping none are
headed your way

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.480
– unless you're a Jayce player...

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.300
...in which case,

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:41.300
f**k you.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.220
[sigh]

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.280
Okay. How was that?

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.260
???: Pretty good, but...

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:46.920
[Nerf gun being loaded]

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.120
Hasbro send their regards.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.220
Announcer: You got Nerfed!

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.420
Miles: Do you want to see it?

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:20.960
Nav: I have eyedrops...

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:21.480
[Josh laughs]

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.480
Josh: Do I want to see it?! Yes, I want to see it!

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.660
Nav: Once his vision comes back he'll see it.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.780
[laughter]

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.980
It really hurt!

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.300
It really hurt!

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.540
Nav: Are you okay?

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.540
Josh: Yeah, I'm fine...

